@page "/how-it-works"

<PageTitle>How It Works - BasketElo</PageTitle>

<div class="method-page be-story">
    <header class="be-story-hero be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 0">
        <div class="be-story-hero-copy">
            <p class="be-story-kicker">How BasketElo works</p>
            <h1 class="be-story-title">A rating that changes as the evidence changes.</h1>
            <p class="be-story-lede">Every game becomes a small correction to what the model thought it knew. The method is simple enough to explain, but explicit enough to question.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="be-story-orbit" aria-hidden="true">
            <div class="be-story-orbit-core">
                <span>Public ruleset</span>
                <strong>± ELO</strong>
                <small>updates after every result</small>
            </div>
        </div>
    </header>

    <section class="be-story-section be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 1" aria-labelledby="history-heading">
        <div class="be-story-meta"><span class="be-story-index">01</span><span>A short history</span></div>
        <div class="be-story-body">
            <h2 id="history-heading" class="be-story-heading">From chess boards to basketball courts.</h2>
            <div class="be-story-copy method-copy-columns">
                <p>ELO was developed by Arpad Elo, a physicist and chess player, for the World Chess Federation. The idea was practical: give every player a rating, compare those ratings before a match, then update them using the result.</p>
                <p>A win against a highly rated opponent tells us more than a routine win against a lower-rated one. Over time, ratings become a running record of performance relative to the field.</p>
                <p>Basketball adds margins, home courts, leagues, seasons, and uneven data. BasketElo keeps the original idea, then makes those basketball-specific choices visible.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="be-story-section be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 2" aria-labelledby="rulesets-heading">
        <div class="be-story-meta"><span class="be-story-index">02</span><span>Different lenses</span></div>
        <div class="be-story-body">
            <h2 id="rulesets-heading" class="be-story-heading">The difference is in what a game tells the model.</h2>
            <p class="be-story-copy method-section-intro">There is no single universally correct way to apply ELO to basketball. We keep the choices explicit so they can be tested, compared, and improved.</p>
            <div class="ruleset-grid">
                <article class="ruleset-item"><span class="ruleset-tag">Simple baseline</span><h3>Basic ELO</h3><p>A plain win/loss model. Easy to understand, and a useful reference point for every other experiment.</p></article>
                <article class="ruleset-item"><span class="ruleset-tag">Margin-aware</span><h3>Point-margin ELO</h3><p>A close win and a large win carry different amounts of information, so score margin strengthens or softens the normal movement.</p></article>
                <article class="ruleset-item is-public"><span class="ruleset-tag">Public default</span><h3>Adjusted ELO</h3><p>The calibrated public version: margin adjustment, a 70-point home advantage, and documented constants for every public rating.</p></article>
            </div>
            <aside class="method-inline-note"><strong>These are modelling choices, not laws of basketball.</strong><span>Keeping rulesets separate lets us compare them honestly instead of quietly changing the numbers underneath.</span></aside>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="be-story-section be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 3" aria-labelledby="formula-heading">
        <div class="be-story-meta"><span class="be-story-index">03</span><span>One game</span></div>
        <div class="be-story-body">
            <h2 id="formula-heading" class="be-story-heading">What the numbers are doing.</h2>
            <p class="be-story-copy method-section-intro">A simplified example using the public adjusted ruleset shows the shape of the calculation without hiding its assumptions behind notation.</p>
            <div class="formula-track">
                <article><span>01 / Home edge</span><code>1,600 + 70 − 1,500 = 170</code><p>The home team begins with an effective difference of 170 points.</p></article>
                <article><span>02 / Expectation</span><code>170-point gap → about 73%</code><p>On a 400-point probability scale, the home side is expected to win roughly 73% of the time.</p></article>
                <article><span>03 / Update</span><code>20 × (1 − 0.73) = +5.4</code><p>A win adds about 5.4 points before margin and competition weight are applied.</p></article>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="be-story-section be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 4" aria-labelledby="levers-heading">
        <div class="be-story-meta"><span class="be-story-index">04</span><span>Model levers</span></div>
        <div class="be-story-body">
            <h2 id="levers-heading" class="be-story-heading">The assumptions an experiment can move.</h2>
            <p class="be-story-copy method-section-intro">Model Lab exposes the parameters that can reasonably vary between leagues or questions.</p>
            <div class="lever-list">
                <article><span>01</span><div><h3>K-factor</h3><p>How quickly a rating reacts. The public starting point is 20; higher values follow recent form more closely.</p></div></article>
                <article><span>02</span><div><h3>Home advantage</h3><p>How much home court contributes before the result is evaluated. The public ruleset uses 70 ELO points.</p></div></article>
                <article><span>03</span><div><h3>Probability scale</h3><p>How large a rating gap must be before an expected result becomes strongly one-sided. The current scale is 400.</p></div></article>
                <article><span>04</span><div><h3>Point-margin scale</h3><p>How an ELO gap translates into expected scoring margin. The current scale is 28 points per ELO margin.</p></div></article>
                <article><span>05</span><div><h3>Margin dampener and cap</h3><p>How quickly margin matters, and how far it can amplify a normal update without letting one blowout dominate.</p></div></article>
                <article><span>06</span><div><h3>Competition weight</h3><p>Whether results from one competition move the shared rating pool more or less. It is currently 1.0.</p></div></article>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="be-story-section be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 5" aria-labelledby="implementation-heading">
        <div class="be-story-meta"><span class="be-story-index">05</span><span>Versioning</span></div>
        <div class="be-story-body method-version-block">
            <h2 id="implementation-heading" class="be-story-heading">The implementation is versioned, too.</h2>
            <div class="be-story-copy"><p>Every rating is tied to a ruleset version. A history can be rebuilt with a new formula without silently mixing it with ratings produced by an older one. Inputs, scope, seasons, and parameters stay reviewable alongside the result.</p></div>
            <a class="method-text-link" href="model-lab">Explore Model Lab <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="be-story-section be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 6" aria-labelledby="identity-nightmare-heading">
        <div class="be-story-meta"><span class="be-story-index">06</span><span>Case file</span></div>
        <div class="be-story-body identity-case">
            <p class="identity-case-label">Identity investigation / Serbia</p>
            <h2 id="identity-nightmare-heading" class="be-story-heading">The Radnički continuity nightmare.</h2>
            <div class="identity-case-copy">
                <p>Team identity is often harder than the rating formula. One provider may reuse a name or ID across decades while historical sources distinguish clubs that share a city and sponsor.</p>
                <p>Radnički is a good example. One catch-all record held 755 games belonging to several clubs. Timelines, aliases, competitions, seasons, and venue evidence had to be considered together. PearlBasket’s Belgrade venue evidence supported <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BKK_Radni%C4%8Dki">BKK Radnički</a>, while <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KK_Radni%C4%8Dki_KG_06">Radnički KG 06</a> accounted for the 2006–07 and 2008–09 BIBL games.</p>
                <p>The correction moved 328 games to Radnički Beograd, 32 to KG 06, 243 to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KK_Radni%C4%8Dki_Kragujevac_(2009%E2%80%932014)">Radnički Kragujevac (2009–2014)</a>, and 152 to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KK_SPD_Radni%C4%8Dki">SPD Radnički (2015–present)</a>.</p>
                <p>A shared name is not proof of continuity, and a source ID is not a historical club identity. Provenance and season boundaries matter because a refounded club may share a city, colours, and name while still needing a fresh rating history.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <aside class="be-story-note be-reveal" style="--reveal-order: 7">
        <strong>A rating is a useful signal, not a verdict.</strong>
        <span>It describes what the results support right now. Injuries, line-ups, travel, motivation, and future change can all sit outside the model.</span>
    </aside>
</div>
